# WCAG 2.1 AA Accessibility Requirements

## Overview

Every component and page in this project must meet **WCAG 2.1 Level AA**. Carbon
Design System components are accessible by default — preserve that accessibility
when composing them, and apply the patterns below whenever you write custom
components or override Carbon markup.

---

## 1. Global `.sr-only` Utility Class

A `.sr-only` class is defined globally in `src/styles/components.scss`. Use it
to add screen-reader-only text without cluttering the visual UI.


/* already in src/styles/components.scss — do not redefine */
.sr-only {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  padding: 0;
  margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
  white-space: nowrap;
  border: 0;
}
```

```jsx
// Usage
<Tag type="green" renderIcon={CheckmarkFilled}>
  {step.label}
  <span className="sr-only"> — complete</span>
</Tag>
```

---

## 2. WCAG 1.4.1 — Not Color or Symbol Alone

Never rely solely on color, an emoji, or a shape to communicate meaning.
Always pair a visual indicator with a text alternative.

### Emoji in tables / comparison grids
```jsx
// ❌ BAD — emoji is the only indicator
<td>✅</td>

// ✅ GOOD — emoji is decorative, sr-only carries the meaning
function ScoreCell({ score }) {
  const map = {
    '✅': { label: 'Yes', cls: 'score--yes' },
    '❌': { label: 'No',  cls: 'score--no'  },
    '⚠️': { label: 'Partial', cls: 'score--partial' },
  };
  const meta = map[score] ?? { label: score, cls: '' };
  return (
    <span className={`score ${meta.cls}`}>
      <span aria-hidden="true">{score}</span>
      <span className="sr-only">{meta.label}</span>
    </span>
  );
}
```

### Status icons (Carbon icons used as status)
```jsx
// ❌ BAD — icon communicates state visually only
<CheckmarkFilled size={16} className="icon--done" />

// ✅ GOOD — sr-only text accompanies the icon
<CheckmarkFilled size={16} className="icon--done" />
<span className="sr-only">complete</span>
```

---

## 3. Decorative Elements — `aria-hidden="true"`

Any element that is purely visual and adds no information for screen readers
must be hidden from the accessibility tree.

```jsx
// ✅ Decorative number badge (heading already names the section)
<div className="option-number" aria-hidden="true">1</div>

// ✅ Decorative mobile phone chrome in a preview component
<div className="mobile-frame-notch" aria-hidden="true" />
<div className="mobile-frame-status-bar" aria-hidden="true">
  <span>9:41</span>
  <span>●●●</span>
</div>

// ✅ Decorative connector line between steps
<div className="step-connector" aria-hidden="true" />
```

**Important**: Do NOT apply `aria-hidden="true"` to a parent element that
contains meaningful interactive or text content — it hides the entire subtree.

---

## 4. Semantic HTML — Tables

Use real `<table>` elements when displaying tabular comparison data. Do not use
CSS-grid divs as a fake table — screen readers cannot navigate them.

```jsx
// ❌ BAD — divs with grid layout are not accessible as a table
<div className="comparison-header">
  <div>Criteria</div>
  <div>Option 1</div>
</div>
<div className="comparison-row">
  <div>No scroll</div>
  <div>✅</div>
</div>

// ✅ GOOD — semantic table with scope attributes
<table aria-label="Design comparison">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Criteria</th>
      <th scope="col">Option 1</th>
      <th scope="col">Option 2</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>No scroll</td>
      <td><ScoreCell score="✅" /></td>
      <td><ScoreCell score="❌" /></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

---

## 5. ARIA Roles and Landmarks

### Page structure
- Use `<main>` (not `<div>`) for the page content root
- Use `<section aria-labelledby="heading-id">` for named regions
- Use `<nav aria-label="...">` for navigation blocks

```jsx
// ✅ Page root
<main className="pip-page">

// ✅ Named section
<section className="pip-option" aria-labelledby="opt1-title">
  <h2 id="opt1-title">Vertical Step Tracker</h2>
  ...
</section>

// ✅ Interactive region
<div role="region" aria-label="Step preview controls">
  ...
</div>
```

### Custom progress / stepper components
Do not use `role="progressbar"` on a container that also has child elements with
individual `aria-label`s — this creates redundant/conflicting announcements.

```jsx
// ❌ BAD — role="progressbar" + per-dot aria-labels = noisy/conflicting
<div role="progressbar" aria-valuenow={2} aria-valuemin={1} aria-valuemax={6}>
  {steps.map(step => (
    <div aria-label={`${step.label}: current`} />
  ))}
</div>

// ✅ GOOD — single aria-label on wrapper, visual track is aria-hidden,
//           a hidden <ol> gives screen readers the full step list
<div
  role="group"
  aria-label={`Progress: step ${current + 1} of ${total}, ${steps[current].label}`}
>
  <div aria-hidden="true">
    {/* visual dots / track */}
  </div>
  <ol className="sr-only">
    {steps.map((s, i) => (
      <li key={s.key}>{s.label}: {i < current ? 'complete' : i === current ? 'current' : 'upcoming'}</li>
    ))}
  </ol>
</div>
```

